  "state": {
    "case_id": "159",
    "case_summary": "A 56-year-old nulliparous woman presented 14 months after a supracervical laparoscopic hysterectomy and bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy for menorrhagia, pelvic pain, and large uterine leiomyomas. Her preoperative evaluation included a normal Pap smear; no chest radiograph or endometrial biopsy was obtained. At surgery, an 18–20-week–size uterus (1518 g) was removed via prolonged mechanical morcellation without a containment bag; superficial pelvic endometriosis was noted and cauterized. Final pathology on six uterine fragments demonstrated weakly proliferative endometrium without atypia, adenomyosis, and leiomyoma; the ovaries showed benign parenchyma and endosalpingiosis. \n\nOn presentation, she reported left lower quadrant pain, persistent cough, and shortness of breath. Examination revealed a firm left lower quadrant abdominal mass and a fleshy lesion at the vaginal apex; the cervix was not visualized. PET–CT showed multiple hypermetabolic masses in the pelvis, abdomen, and chest. A core-needle biopsy of a 9 cm left pelvic mass revealed poorly differentiated epithelioid cells with hyperchromatic nuclei in necrosis. Immunohistochemical stains were positive for cytokeratin, vimentin, and CD138, and negative for SMA, EMA, ER, TLE1, HMB45, desmin, myogenin, CD117, BCL2, inhibin, MDN2, CD4, CA125, WT-1, CD45, calretinin, DOG-1, p16, CEA, CK20, HPV, and CD99. A vaginal biopsy showed grade 3 endometrioid adenocarcinoma. Pathology re-review of the hysterectomy and oophorectomy specimens showed no evidence of malignancy.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. endometrioid adenocarcinoma\nB. Recurrent uterine cancer was considered given the possibility of an occult malignancy at hysterectomy\nC. Primary peritoneal carcinoma arising from endometriosis was considered because of documented pelvic endometriosis at\nD. Malignant transformation of retained endometrial tissue or endometriosis after morcellation was considered as an\nE. None\nF. None\nG. None\nH. None\n",
